WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.699
It can be tough for a movie studio to know
when they've got a flop on their hands until

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:04.300
it's too late.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.010
But once in awhile, films come along that
have DISASTER written all over them in big,

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.599
flashing neon letters, long before they're
released.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.400
The writing was on the wall for these pictures
well before they made their way to theaters.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.700
They're some of the biggest box office bombs
of all time, and many of us saw them coming

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:20.620
from a mile away.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.180
Aeon Flux

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.260
The live-action adaptation of MTV's animated
series Aeon Flux, with Charlize Theron in

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.540
the title role, arrived a full decade after
the last episode of the show aired.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.880
Already facing that uphill battle, the film
was shuffled around through multiple regime

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:36.920
changes at Paramount.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:41.280
Studio head Sherry Lansing initially told
director Karyn Kusama that she wanted it to

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.340
be Paramount's answer to Blade Runner, but
incoming chief Gail Berman wanted more of

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.080
a Buffy the Vampire Slayer vibe.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:52.040
After struggling through the shoot with unclear
directives, Kusama then endured the ultimate

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:56.340
insult as a third incoming administration
removed her from the post-production process,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.040
and, in her words, "eviscerated" the film.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.080
“Now when I reflect back on it I’m like,
‘What was I thinking?’”

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:08.340
About 25 minutes was sliced from Kusama's
cut, resulting in a barely coherent movie;

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.720
Kusama would later say,

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:14.300
"What they didn't realize is that sometimes
a cohesive 100 minutes will feel much shorter

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.340
than an incoherent and barely comprehensible
82."

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.540
It predictably garnered appalling reviews
and barely registered at the box office.

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:23.800
Cowboys and Aliens

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.300
Cowboys and Aliens seemed like the kind of
high-concept romp that the right filmmaker

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.229
could knock out of the park, and director
Jon Favreau, fresh off the success of Iron

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:35.240
Man and its sequel, certainly seemed like
the man for the job.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:40.450
With Steven Spielberg as a producer and an
all-star cast including Daniel Craig and Harrison

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:44.940
Ford, big things were in store for the picture,
but they missed a couple of red flags.

00:01:45.369 --> 00:01:50.460
The script was labored over by more than a
dozen writers for 14 years, which is never

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:50.960
a good sign.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:56.390
It also tracked poorly with its target audience
for the duration of its long marketing campaign.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:57.500
The biggest insult of all?

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.080
It just barely managed to edge out The Smurfs
in its opening week.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.660
"I'm so sorry, I didn't realize.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:04.660
How sad for you."

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:08.160
It ultimately failed to make back its production
budget domestically.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.340
Cowboys and Aliens certainly had a wealth
of talent, but everyone involved forgot the

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:16.620
crucial step of making sure they were crafting
a picture people actually wanted to see.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.320
“Ask him about Cowboys and Aliens…”

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:21.780
Fantastic Four

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.430
Production problems are a pretty reliable
indicator that a big-budget tentpole film

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:32.180
could be in trouble, and few have had production
issues as extensive as 2015's Fantastic Four.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:37.240
From questionable casting, to clashes between
director Josh Trank and writer-producer Simon

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:42.250
Kinberg, rumors flew fast and furious about
the snafus plaguing Fantastic Four for months

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:43.600
before the film's release.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.120
Trank and Kinberg publicly shot down these
rumors, but then the film hit theaters, and

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.460
it became astoundingly clear that every single
rumor had been true, and then some.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:56.720
Audiences stayed away, and those who gave
it a chance mostly wished they hadn't.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:01.200
In the wake of the hostile reviews, Trank
defended his work via Twitter by implying

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.420
that the studio had savaged the film in editing.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.660
As for Kinberg, he ignored the film's troubled
production history by placing the blame for

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:11.879
its failure on its tone, proving that some
people fail to learn lessons even when they're

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:12.820
taught the hard way.

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:14.700
John Carter

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:17.660
Disney's John Carter had a lot of things going
for it.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:22.310
It was the start of an adaptation of a series
of classic, influential sci-fi novels by Edgar

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:26.939
Rice Burroughs, and director Andrew Stanton
had previous hits with Pixar including Wall-E

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:28.020
and Finding Nemo.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:31.800
And it had the massive production budget one
would expect for a tentpole film.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.640
Any or all of these things would have been
brilliant to play up in the film's marketing,

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:39.600
so of course the first trailer capitalized
by mentioning exactly none of them.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:45.200
The teaser appeared in July 2011, and it wasn't
until the 2012 Super Bowl that a second trailer

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:45.900
was released.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:47.860
Unfortunately, it wasn't much better.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:52.250
Speaking with Vulture, an anonymous former
studio executive called John Carter's marketing

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:54.300
campaign the worst in history, saying,

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:57.299
"It's almost as if they went out of their
way to not make us care."

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:02.740
Amazingly, the problem was compounded by Stanton's
complete loyalty to the source material, assuming

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:05.920
that the public at large would adore John
Carter as much as he did.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.300
“You are ugly but you are beautiful.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:12.300
And you fight like a Thark.”

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:17.080
This colossal miscalculation plus the vague
marketing campaign equaled very few butts

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.840
in the seats, and Carter became far and away
the biggest flop of 2012.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:23.000
The Lone Ranger

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:28.020
When Disney's live-action The Lone Ranger
was announced in 2010, it wasn't clear whether

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:31.390
audiences were up for a retelling of the adventures
of the Western hero.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.520
Plus, a few feathers were ruffled over the
choices of Armie Hammer in the title role

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.140
and the decidedly non-Native Johnny Depp as
his Native American sidekick Tonto.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.930
The production was briefly shut down over
budget concerns in 2011, but managed to get

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:45.700
back on track.

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.740
Then, in early 2012, fans were treated to
the first glimpse of the film, featuring Hammer

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:55.800
in the famous mask…and Depp in the most
egregiously terrible Tonto makeup anyone could

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.300
have imagined.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:01.310
The images quickly drew jeers, giving observers
plenty of negative things to talk about before

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:04.160
the film was finally released in July 2013.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:08.960
It failed to generate any significant positive
social media buzz, and analysts correctly

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:12.930
predicted that the film would be trounced
by Despicable Me 2, which opened the same

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.849
weekend and went on to gross nearly a billion
dollars.

